  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing emergency health needs and humanitarian border management during conflict</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Despite ongoing diplomatic efforts, the conflict in Sudan has persisted since April 2023, evolving into the largest and most catastrophic displacement, humanitarian, and protection crisis in the world today. The humanitarian situation is dire, with 30.4 million people now needing assistance&#x2014;half of Sudan&apos;s population&#x2014;up from 15.8 million in 2022. The crisis is further complicated by extreme weather events, including heavy rains and flooding in 2024, which displaced almost 200,000 people across 15 states.This proposal aims to improve basic service provision of life-saving humanitarian assistance in key border areas, including with Chad, Egypt, and Ethiopia. Building on the current Japan-funded project, this is being achieved through a two-pronged approach consisting of: 1) improving the existing primary healthcare centres (PHCs) near international borders and internally displaced persons (IDP) gathering points with integrated health and nutrition services ; and 2) strengthening the capacity of  border officials  to facilitate the arrival  and reception of critical humanitarian resources into Sudan, at select points of entry (PoEs).</narrative>
  </description>
